{
 "source": "PC NFH2, the anger gauge's jumps in Badinfos' 100 % run (tools/pcref/amounts.py: the jump per second scaled to the full bar, 92 % of the crop, labelled with the HUD bubble's activity) — a jump that hits the full bar is a lower bound and keeps the mobile value where that is higher; a jump shared by linked coins is split in the mobile proportions; an unmeasured coin keeps the mobile value. E07 Mummy's Darling: +19 the board (123 s), +25 the elephant (170), +25 the shell (193), +25 and +25 the castle with its towel (221, 225), +32 at the full bar in his second elephant visit — the awning (239), +12 on the full bar (279). PC data (in_b1/tricks.xml, rage per trick in thousandths of the gauge): the mobile AngerAmount values are the PC's but for the sand castle: its own record is the kid's splash_crayfish 20 (SandCastle 20; the mobile's 40), and with the hedgehog's towel the linked flow pays mat_hedgehog 20 (the towel's) and the billboard's bill 30 (PCExtraCoinLinked, the mobile's extra 10) — 70 either way (tools/pcref/lap_model_s2.py LINKED_CONT; 2026-09-24). The gauge jumps read off Badinfos' video (2026-09-08) ran 10-25 %% high and are withdrawn. The gauge is 100 000 rage long — the mobile's AngryMeterMaximum 100: the PC dialog (nfh2 dialogs/*/menuleft_bar.xml) gives the rageometer range 0..100000 and GameLogic.dll flags 100000 (docs/PC_ROUTINES.md); the 80 read off the video was the bar's geometry, as the Season 1 tube's was. Its decay is the mobile's 0.37 %/s: the bar falls 41 -> 11 % over 70 trick-free seconds of E01 (tools/pcref/gauge.py, x0.8 for the crop). The decay is leveldata.xml's time=\"30\" per 1/12 s tick (GameLogic.dll's level update 0x100442b3, 0x100447c4: rage -= the level record's +0x28, the app's leveldata parser stores `time` there; the HUD clock divides the tick count by 12) — PCRageDecay 30, 0.36 %/s against the mobile's 0.37. The reactions (tools/pcref/coins.py --write-laugh): PCLaugh = the `<trick laugh=>` level of the records the item's trick fires, which picks the PC neighbour's reaction clip (pcprofile.S2_REACTION_CLIPS). The catch (tools/pcref/pc_catch_s2.py): PCHideout on the catchers' stations = per role the span of the PC's flag 4 (the enter step to the leave step, less the level steps' clears), in which the neighbour or the Mother neither catches nor is seen. The door passes (tools/pcref/pc_walks_s2.py): PCPass on each Transition = per pawn the PC pass of the door pair it stands for — the near door's <actor>_in against the room's floor, the straight movement to the far door's <actor>_out (or the enter + leave ticks), the far floor against <actor>_out, the x of both hotspots, in px of the PC scene; PCApproach on the actors' stations and Woody's items = per role the PC object's <actor> hotspot x and its height against the room's floor (tx: the neighbour's move over the station's actions, their <translation>s; txt: after a tricked visit); PCRoom on each Zone = its PC room's floor line and <neighbor> records with the doors' <actor> hotspots, for the path finder's routes. Station durations (tools/pcref/pc_durations_s2.py): GameLogic.dll's level script (tools/pcref/lap_model_s2.py code_stays: the untricked lap's actions, hideouts and bars per mobile item), the PC video bubble spans of docs/PC_LAPS_DETAIL.md less the walk for the items the model leaves untimed, PCUseSeconds per visit. Another actor's clips at the PC's ticks (tools/pcref/pc_durations_others.py CLIPS_ROLE): PCClipSecondsRole, the level data's actions and clips paired by hand. The mini-game (tools/pcref/pc_minigames.py): PCMinigameTicks = the `time` of the game object's Woody action in objects.xml, PCMinigameLevels = the startlevel and endlevel of its combine.xml combination (the wobble's range), PCMinigameFailed = the behavioractor of its `failed` action (the actor a lost game sends running), PCMinigameFailedClip = the clip that actor's behaviour plays first when it is not the neighbour (203's Olga shouts, 69 frames, and her shout's own behaviour is his run), PCMinigameLift = the height in level px of Woody's place for the game (the object's `woody` hotspot) above its room's floor line, the field's middle 150 px above it.",
